WEBVTT

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:31.810
<v Ira Glass>There's this scene in a book I love, About a Boy , by Nick Hornby. The situation in the scene is this. There's this very well-meaning guy who's decided that a good way to meet women would be to join a group for single parents. He's not a single parent himself. He has no children at all. But this guy, whose name is Will, goes to a meeting and gets caught up in conversations. He finds himself making up all sorts of things about his non-existent child and non-existent ex-wife.
<v Ira Glass>我喜爱的一本书里有这样一个场景，尼克·霍恩比（Nick Hornby）写的《单亲插班生》（About a Boy）。场景里的情况是这样的：一个心地非常善良的男人，认为结识女性的一个好办法就是加入一个单亲家长互助组。但他自己并不是单亲家长，他根本没有孩子。不过，这个名叫威尔（Will）的男人还是去参加了会议，并卷入了谈话。他发现自己开始编造各种关于他并不存在的孩子和并不存在的前妻的事情。

00:00:31.810 --> 00:01:11.330
<v Ira Glass>There's a tall, blond woman named Susie at the meeting. She's nervy-looking. She's beautiful. They chat. They make plans to meet at a picnic for kids and parents. And, comes the day of, Will calls Susie to tell her that his son, Ned, will not be there. Now, Ned will not be there because Ned does not exist, but Will tells Susie that Ned won't be there because his mom has taken him for the day, at the last minute. Susie tells Will that Will needs to stand up to his non-existent ex-wife a little more firmly. And then there's this passage, which I'm going to read.
<v Ira Glass>会议上有一位高个子的金发女性，名叫苏西（Susie）。她看起来有些紧张，但很漂亮。他们聊了起来，并商定在一次儿童与家长的野餐会上见面。到了那天，威尔给苏西打电话，告诉她他的儿子内德（Ned）不会去了。其实内德不会去是因为内德根本不存在，但威尔告诉苏西，是因为内德的妈妈临时把孩子接走了。苏西告诉威尔，他对那个不存在的前妻应该更强硬一点。接着，就是我要读的这一段。

00:01:11.330 --> 00:02:06.650
<v Ira Glass>It was much more confusing than Will had imagined, making people up, and he was beginning to realize that he hadn't thought it through properly at all. How could he carry it through? How many times could Ned reasonably be whisked away by his mother, or maternal grandmother, or international terrorists? What reasons could he give for not inviting Susie round to his flat where there were no toys, or cribs, or diapers, or balls, and where there was no second bedroom even? Could he kill Ned off with some awful disease? A car crash? Tragic. Tragic. Life goes on. Maybe not. Parents got pretty cut up about kids dying, and he'd find the requisite years of grief a real drain on his thespian resources. No, disaster was approaching, and there was nothing he can do about it. Best pull out now. Walk away. But walking away wasn't Will's style. He always felt something would turn up, even though nothing ever did, or even could most the time.
<v Ira Glass>编造谎言比威尔想象的要复杂得多，他开始意识到自己根本没考虑周全。他该怎么把这个谎圆下去？内德的母亲，或者外祖母，甚至国际恐怖分子，可以把内德带走多少次？他能给出什么理由不邀请苏西到他的公寓去呢？那里没有玩具、婴儿床、尿布或球，甚至连第二间卧室都没有。他能让内德死于某种可怕的疾病吗？车祸？太悲剧了。太悲剧了。生活还要继续。也许不行。父母对孩子的死亡会非常痛苦，而他觉得去应付几年必需的悲伤，对他那点表演功底来说简直是巨大的消耗。不，灾难即将降临，他束手无策。最好现在就退出，走人。但走人不是威尔的风格。他总是觉得会有转机，尽管大多数时候根本没有什么转机，也不可能有什么转机。

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:30.120
<v Ira Glass>Once, years ago, when he was a kid, he told a school friend, having first ascertained that this friend was not a CS Lewis fan, that it was possible to walk through the back of his wardrobe into a different world, and invited him around to explore. He could have canceled. He could have told him anything. But he was not prepared to suffer a moment's mild embarrassment if there was no immediate need to do so.
<v Ira Glass>很久以前，还是个孩子的时候，有一次他告诉一个校友——在确认对方不是C·S·路易斯的粉丝后——说穿过他衣柜的背面就能进入另一个世界，还邀请对方去探险。他本可以取消的，他本可以找任何借口。但他不想在没有迫切需要的情况下，去承受哪怕一丁点儿轻微的尴尬。

00:02:30.120 --> 00:03:06.390
<v Ira Glass>And so the two of them scrabbled around among the coat hangers for several minutes until Will mumbled something about the world being closed on Saturday afternoons. The thing was, he could still remember feeling genuinely hopeful right up to the last minute. Maybe there will be something here, he had thought. Maybe I won't lose face. There wasn't, and he did, loads of it, a whole head full of face. But he hadn't learned a thing from the experience. If anything, it seemed to have left him with the feeling that he was bound to be lucky next time.
<v Ira Glass>于是，他们俩在衣架之间翻找了几分钟，直到威尔嘟囔着说那个世界周六下午关门。关键是，直到最后一刻，他依然能感觉到自己是真心怀着希望的。他当时想，也许那里真的会有什么东西。也许我就不用丢脸了。结果什么都没有，他也丢尽了脸，丢得彻彻底底。但他从这次经历中一点教训也没学到。如果说有什么改变的话，那就是这次经历反而让他觉得，下次他一定会走运。

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:32.810
<v Ira Glass>As this scene makes clear, there are different kinds of deception. And the line between deceiving others and deceiving yourself can get kind of hazy, especially when hope comes into play. Today on our program, the three kinds of deception-- at least we're claiming that there are only three for the purposes of this radio show-- the three kinds of deception and how any one of them so easily turns into any one of the others.
<v Ira Glass>正如这个场景所展示的，欺骗有多种形式。欺骗他人与欺骗自己之间的界限可能会变得相当模糊，尤其是在涉及希望的时候。今天在我们的节目中，我们将讨论三种欺骗——至少为了这期广播节目，我们声称只有这三种——这三种欺骗以及它们中的任何一种是如何轻易转化为另一种的。

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:55.680
<v Ira Glass>Act One of our program, Self-Deception, the story of an ex-con who not only told himself a lot of things that were untrue, he created a sort of web of unreality around himself so that dozens of people in a small town started kidding themselves about what was real and what was not real. And then, all of them together, decided to make a feature film.
<v Ira Glass>节目的第一幕，自欺欺人，讲述了一个前罪犯的故事，他不仅对自己说了许多不实之词，还在自己周围编织了一张虚假的网，让一个小镇上的几十个人开始自欺欺人，分不清现实与虚幻。然后，他们所有人竟然决定一起拍摄一部故事片。

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:12.490
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, Deceiving Others, an African-American lawyer in a fancy Manhattan firm decides that he wants to see what life is like in the exclusive country clubs that his colleagues go to. So he enters one the only way that they would let a black man like him in.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，欺骗他人，一位在曼哈顿一家高级律师事务所工作的非裔美国律师，决定去看看他的同事们经常出入的高级乡村俱乐部到底是什么样子的。于是，他用唯一一种能让像他这样的黑人进入的方式混了进去。

00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:40.430
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, Accidental Deception, American tourists in Paris accidentally mistake writer David Sedaris for a French pickpocket. At first it makes him mad, and then he starts to kind of like it. From WBEZ Chicago and Public Radio International, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，意外的欺骗，美国游客在巴黎意外地把作家大卫·塞达里斯（David Sedaris）误当作法国扒手。起初他很生气，但后来他居然开始喜欢这种感觉了。这里是来自WBEZ芝加哥和国际公共广播电台的《美国生活》（This American Life）。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。请继续收听。

00:04:40.430 --> 00:05:07.510
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Self-Deception. It's a truism that a great salesman has to actually believe the lies he tells to make the sale, the way a great actor has to believe in his own part. This next story is about somebody who aspires to be a great actor, but, in fact, is probably just a great salesman, a charmer in the old school. Alex Blumberg has the story of how he convinced an entire town a bunch of things that, on deeper examination, were more hope than truth.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，自欺欺人。一个公认的事实是，优秀的推销员必须真正相信自己推销的谎言才能完成交易，就像优秀的演员必须相信自己的角色一样。接下来的这个故事是关于一个渴望成为伟大演员的人，但事实上，他可能只是一个伟大的推销员，一个老派的魅力男。亚历克斯·布隆伯格（Alex Blumberg）将讲述他如何让整个小镇相信了一堆事情，而这些事情在深入考察后发现，比起事实，更多的是一种愿望。

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:23.370
<v Alex Blumberg>Of all the tall tales Richard Castellano told, the most incredible one was the easiest to actually verify. He had been in a film with Robert De Niro and Billy Crystal called Analyze This. He had a total of 16 lines. Almost all of them were one or two sentences long. He sounded like this.
<v Alex Blumberg>在理查德·卡斯特拉诺（Richard Castellano）讲述的所有荒诞故事中，最不可思议的一个其实是最容易核实的。他曾参演过罗伯特·德尼罗（Robert De Niro）和比利·克里斯托（Billy Crystal）主演的电影《老大靠边闪》（Analyze This）。他一共只有16句台词，几乎每句都只有一两句话长。他听起来是这样的。

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:25.850
<v Richard Castellano>What are you, some kind of moron?
<v Richard Castellano>你是什么人，白痴吗？

00:05:25.850 --> 00:05:54.480
<v Alex Blumberg>This is the story of the power that 16 little lines like that can have, how those 16 lines can be parlayed into money, and excitement, and hope for lots of people. And it starts shortly after Richard Castellano finished filming Analyze This and moved with his wife to a tiny community in upstate New York where there's one main street, called Main Street, one block long, and one bridge across the Delaware River to Pennsylvania. It's called Narrowsburg and, with a population in the hundreds, it's, literally, a hamlet.
<v Alex Blumberg>这就是那16句小台词所拥有的力量的故事，这些台词是如何被用来换取金钱、兴奋和许多人的希望的。这一切始于理查德·卡斯特拉诺拍完《老大靠边闪》之后，带着妻子搬到了纽约州北部的一个小社区。那里只有一条主街，就叫主街（Main Street），全长只有一个街区，还有一座横跨特拉华河通往宾夕法尼亚州的桥。这个地方叫纳罗斯堡（Narrowsburg），人口只有几百人，名副其实是一个小村庄。

00:05:54.480 --> 00:06:01.600
<v Dick Crandell>There's no population requirement to be a hamlet. If it were bigger than that, it would be a village, and we don't have a village because it's not enough people.
<v Dick Crandell>成为村庄对人口没有要求。如果比这大一点，它就会成为一个镇，但我们没有成为镇，因为人还不够多。

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:15.870
<v Alex Blumberg>Dick Crandall is the supervisor of Narrowsburg. Supervisor's what you call a mayor when a town's too small to have mayors. And he remembers when Castellano-- Richie, as he came to be called-- first moved to town. In fact, everyone here has a story.
<v Alex Blumberg>迪克·克兰德尔（Dick Crandall）是纳罗斯堡的督察。督察就是当城镇太小没有市长时对负责人的称呼。他还记得卡斯特拉诺——后来大家叫他里奇（Richie）——刚搬到镇上时的情形。事实上，这里的每个人都有一个关于他的故事。

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:34.040
<v Dick Crandell>He was a kind of a flamboyant personality, absolutely. He wore his dark clothes from New York City and a leather jacket open. And he swashbuckled down the street a little bit, and hollered, and talked to everybody, and, you know, was sort of a conquering hero-type person.
<v Dick Crandell>他绝对是个张扬的人物。他穿着从纽约市带来的深色衣服，敞开着皮夹克。他在街上走起路来有些大摇大摆，大声叫喊，和每个人说话，你知道，就像是一个凯旋归来的英雄。

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:49.300
<v Woman 1>He was larger than life, he absolutely was. He would walk into what was then the Chatterbox Cafe and say that he was buying everybody's breakfast. And he would walk in and say, "Hi. I'm Richard Castellano, and I'm buying breakfast." And then he would walk out.
<v Woman 1>他确实是那种比生活更夸张的人。他会走进当时的“聊天室咖啡馆”（Chatterbox Cafe），说他要请所有人吃早餐。他会走进去说：“嘿，我是理查德·卡斯特拉诺，我请大家吃早餐。”然后他就走掉了。

00:06:49.300 --> 00:07:15.420
<v Man 1>And he liked to sign stuff, and every time I see him he gives me signed stuff of himself, pictures of himself and a bunch of stuff. He signs everything. The first time I went to his office, he had me run over to the restaurant to get him a cup of coffee. And he wrote down what he wanted in the coffee, and he signed that.
<v Man 1>他喜欢给人签名，每次我见到他，他都会给我一些他自己的签名纪念品，他的照片和一堆东西。他什么都签。第一次我去他的办公室时，他让我跑去餐厅给他买杯咖啡。他写下了他想喝的咖啡的要求，然后连那个也签了名。

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:26.110
<v Man 2>He started talking about his checkered background. He didn't hide it. People really liked that story, you know, of a guy who was a con, and look at him now, and he's in a movie with De Niro, you know?
<v Man 2>他开始谈论他那坎坷的背景。他并不掩饰。人们真的很喜欢那个故事，你知道，一个曾经是罪犯的人，看看他现在，他正在和德尼罗一起拍电影，你懂吗？

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:30.890
<v Woman 2>He told people that the name of Narrowsburg was going to be changed to Castellanoville.
<v Woman 2>他告诉人们，纳罗斯堡的名字要改成“卡斯特拉诺维尔”（Castellanoville）。

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:47.900
<v Man 3>He said he heisted a armored car, and he got sent up to Attica. He said to me-- he said it to many people-- that he had $2 million stashed away.
<v Man 3>他说他抢劫了一辆装甲车，然后被送进了阿提卡（Attica）监狱。他对我说——他也对很多人说过——他存了200万美元。

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:50.630
<v Alex Blumberg>From his bank heist?
<v Alex Blumberg>从银行抢劫里得来的？

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:54.390
<v Man 3>Yeah. I mean, he told me he killed somebody.
<v Man 3>对。我是说，他还告诉我他杀过人。

00:07:54.390 --> 00:08:08.130
<v Dick Crandell>That was about the extent of my impression of him, except he called me Bill, which didn't make me too happy because my name is Dick.
<v Dick Crandell>我对他的印象基本就是这样了，除了他管我叫比尔（Bill），这让我很不高兴，因为我叫迪克（Dick）。

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:23.480
<v Alex Blumberg>It isn't easy to get an interview with Richard Castellano. My first instructions were to call, let it ring once, hang up, and call back again so they knew it was me. When we finally do meet, it's in Manhattan in his lawyer's office with his lawyer there.
<v Alex Blumberg>想要采访理查德·卡斯特拉诺可不容易。我的第一条指令是打电话，响一声挂断，然后再打一次，这样他们就知道是我了。当我们最终见面时，是在曼哈顿他律师的办公室里，他的律师也在场。

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:34.270
<v Alex Blumberg>Castellano's in his 50s, but good-looking in that Hollywood way-- slim, well-dressed in expensive blacks and grays, good haircut. He says he understands why everyone in Narrowsburg was so excited to meet him.
<v Alex Blumberg>卡斯特拉诺50多岁，但长着一张好莱坞式的帅脸——身材苗条，穿着昂贵的黑色和灰色衣服，发型整洁。他说他理解为什么纳罗斯堡的每个人见到他都那么兴奋。

00:08:34.270 --> 00:08:52.180
<v Richard Castellano>You know, I've met celebrities myself and I still get that-- what's that word-- I was smitten by people I met in movie theaters. And I remember, when I was a kid, I saw the Three Stooges. They made a personal appearance at the Avon Theater here in Brooklyn. I shook their hands.
<v Richard Castellano>你知道，我自己也见过名人，那种感觉我依然……怎么说呢，我在电影院遇见的人让我非常着迷。我记得小时候看过“三个臭皮匠”（Three Stooges）。他们在布鲁克林的雅芳剧院（Avon Theater）现场露面过。我和他们握了手。

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:53.570
<v Alex Blumberg>How did you feel when you met them?
<v Alex Blumberg>你见到他们时是什么感觉？

00:08:53.570 --> 00:09:08.190
<v Richard Castellano>I'm still talking about it. There must have been some effect, right? So, I'd want to give them a chance. You know what I'm saying? Have a little Hollywood.
<v Richard Castellano>我还在谈论这件事呢。肯定会有某种影响，对吧？所以，我想给他们一个机会。你明白我的意思吗？来点好莱坞的感觉。

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:47.580
<v Alex Blumberg>In the beginning, everyone seemed happy to have a little Hollywood. The town got a charming celebrity with bad boy appeal. The celebrity got an adoring public. Richie opened an acting studio, The Richard Castellano School of Acting, and 42 people signed up for his classes. He and his wife also organized the ambitiously named First Annual Narrowsburg International Independent Film Festival. He said Narrowsburg would soon become the Sundance of the East. But, most exciting, he planned to shoot a movie in Narrowsburg, a gangster picture. Richie himself was the lead. People from the town would fill out the cast. 17-year-old Zac Pontier was one of the ones who showed up at the audition.
<v Alex Blumberg>起初，每个人似乎都很高兴能拥有一点好莱坞的感觉。小镇迎来了一位带有坏小子气质的魅力名人。这位名人得到了一群崇拜他的公众。里奇开了一家表演工作室，叫“理查德·卡斯特拉诺表演学校”，有42人报名参加了他的课程。他和他的妻子还组织了第一届纳罗斯堡国际独立电影节，名字起得雄心勃勃。他说纳罗斯堡很快就会成为东部的圣丹斯（Sundance）。但最让人兴奋的是，他计划在纳罗斯堡拍一部电影，一部黑帮片。里奇本人担任主角，镇上的人将填补其他角色的空缺。17岁的扎克·庞蒂尔（Zac Pontier）就是去参加试镜的人之一。

00:09:47.580 --> 00:10:03.340
<v Zac Pontier>Well, everybody, and all the kids in the town, and everybody that wanted to be an actor, everybody I knew was there, probably, a huge number of people. And he had people come up and improv scenes. Like, he told you what to say.
<v Zac Pontier>嗯，每个人，镇上所有的孩子，所有想成为演员的人，我认识的每个人都在那里，人数非常多。他让人们上台即兴表演场景。比如，他告诉你该说什么。

00:10:03.340 --> 00:10:45.510
<v Zac Pontier>I remember the scene that he had most people do was this kid, Dominic, was yelling at his sister about how he didn't like his father anymore because he felt like his father killed his mother. So, I was a little nervous when I came up and he said, "Action," or whatever he said. And I just went totally off on this one girl, and I was like, "How dare you talk about our mother like that! She's dead! And if it wasn't for our father, then she'd still be alive! And what about me? What do you think about me? I have nobody!" It went on and on like that.
<v Zac Pontier>我记得他让大多数人演的场景是一个叫多米尼克（Dominic）的孩子，他在对他妹妹大喊大叫，说他再也不喜欢他爸爸了，因为他觉得是他爸爸杀了他妈妈。所以我走上去的时候有点紧张，他说“开始”或者别的什么。我完全投入了，对着那个女孩喊道：“你怎么敢那样谈论我们的母亲！她死了！如果不是因为我们的父亲，她现在还活着！那我也算什么？你觉得我是什么？我什么都没有！”诸如此类，一直没停。

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:49.500
<v Alex Blumberg>So, after it was over, did you feel pretty confident about it?
<v Alex Blumberg>所以，表演结束后，你对自己有信心吗？

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:54.210
<v Zac Pontier>Yeah. He told me I did a good job, and he had me put a star by my name.
<v Zac Pontier>有。他告诉我我演得很好，并让我在我的名字旁边画了一颗星。

00:10:54.210 --> 00:10:55.310
<v Alex Blumberg>That had to be a good sign.
<v Alex Blumberg>那一定是个好兆头。

00:10:55.310 --> 00:11:18.340
<v Zac Pontier>Yeah, that was. I was like, oh, star, that's a good sign. I'm good to go. And so, I don't know, I remember that we didn't have call waiting then. And so I was like, "Oh, stay off the phone. No one-- Can't talk on the phone." Like, three weeks after that, I talked on the phone very little.
<v Zac Pontier>是的，确实是。当时我想，哦，星号，这是个好兆头。我有戏了。所以，我不知道，我记得那时我们还没有呼叫等待功能。所以我告诉自己：“哦，别用电话。没人……不能打电话。”接下来的三周，我基本没怎么打电话。

00:11:18.340 --> 00:11:36.360
<v Alex Blumberg>Eventually, Zac got the part. The movie was called Four Deadly Reasons, and shooting began in the spring of '99. And it was the real deal. Giant Italian characters actors with Brooklyn accents wandered around Main Street. People pushing cameras yelled "Cut," and "Action." Guys in vests hollered into walkie-talkies.
<v Alex Blumberg>最终，扎克得到了那个角色。这部电影叫《四个致命理由》（Four Deadly Reasons），拍摄于99年春天开始。这可是来真的。带着布鲁克林口音的意大利巨型角色演员在主街上晃荡。推着摄像机的人大喊“咔”和“开始”。穿着背心的家伙们对着对讲机大吼大叫。

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:51.300
<v Alex Blumberg>Larry Revene, the director of photography, is a 30-year veteran of over 100 low-budget films. He did Hollywood Hot Tubs, Doom Asylum, Bedroom Eyes II . He says, typically, he can shoot a movie in 10 days. The formula always includes either a little T&A, or a little violence.
<v Alex Blumberg>摄影指导拉里·雷文（Larry Revene）是一位拥有30年经验的老手，拍摄过100多部低成本电影。他拍过《好莱坞热水浴缸》（Hollywood Hot Tubs）、《末日收容所》（Doom Asylum）、《卧室之眼2》（Bedroom Eyes II）。他说，通常情况下，他10天就能拍完一部电影。公式总是包括一点卖肉或者一点暴力。

00:11:51.300 --> 00:12:03.740
<v Larry Revene>This [? has no ?] [? pretense ?] to art. This is low-budget, commercial film, you know?
<v Larry Revene>这（？）根本没有任何艺术追求。这就是低成本的商业电影，你懂吗？

00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:19.080
<v Alex Blumberg>Almost immediately, things started going wrong. Castellano was constantly missing from the set. At the end of the first week, the checks he gave the crew all bounced. Stories with headlines like, "Hey, Castellano. Where's My Money?" started appearing in the local paper.
<v Alex Blumberg>几乎立刻，事情就开始出问题了。卡斯特拉诺总是从片场消失。第一周结束时，他发给剧组的所有支票都跳票了。当地报纸开始出现诸如“嘿，卡斯特拉诺，我的钱在哪里？”之类的标题。

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:48.460
<v Alex Blumberg>And all sorts of secrets about him came to light. His name wasn't Castellano like the former crime boss, it was Castaldo. He'd never murdered anyone. And the armored car he claimed to have robbed turned out to be a shoe store. It was all playing out like a very old and very familiar story-- charismatic flim-flam man comes to town, raises people's hopes, rips them off, vanishes. "If only it were so simple," says director of photography Larry Revene. "If only Richard were capable of a con like that."
<v Alex Blumberg>关于他的各种秘密也随之曝光。他根本不是那个犯罪集团头目的那个卡斯特拉诺，他叫卡斯塔尔多（Castaldo）。他从未杀过人。他声称抢劫的那辆装甲车，最后发现其实是一家鞋店。这一切就像一个非常古老且熟悉的故事——魅力十足的骗子进城，吊起人们的胃口，骗光他们的钱，然后消失。摄影指导拉里·雷文说：“如果事情真有那么简单就好了。如果理查德真的有能力策划那样的骗局就好了。”

00:12:48.460 --> 00:13:30.640
<v Larry Revene>Well, I think that that would be giving too much credit to him because I don't think he's capable of that kind of thinking ahead. I think he was thinking, you've got some helmets, I've got some swords, let's go to my garage and we'll put on a play. And he's going to believe it too because it's happening. People are involved. It's all going forward. I hope some money shows up, maybe we can pay everybody. And he's playing out his role as this wonderful movie star.
<v Larry Revene>嗯，我觉得那样评价他太高看他了，因为我觉得他根本没能力进行那种预谋。我想他当时想的是，你们有一些头盔，我有一些剑，咱们去我的车库，演一出戏吧。而且他自己也会相信这是真的，因为事情正在发生。人们参与其中，一切都在进行。我希望会有钱出现，也许我们能付给所有人报酬。他正在扮演他作为一名出色电影明星的角色。

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:41.960
<v Alex Blumberg>17-year-old Zac tells this story. Well into the film, long after the script was done and the parts were cast, Richie was still sending people he'd met to the set, saying that they could get a part in his movie.
<v Alex Blumberg>17岁的扎克讲述了这样一个故事。电影拍摄期间，在剧本早已完成且角色早已分配完毕很久之后，里奇仍然在向他遇见的每一个人推荐去片场，说他们能在他的电影里得到一个角色。

00:13:41.960 --> 00:14:05.080
<v Zac Pontier>And this would happen all the time. Every time the town coordinator came outside onto the porch, there would be more people there, like, "Are you the town coordinator? We're looking for the town coordinator. Richie said that we had a big part in his movie. In the scene by the pool, I'm the guy that's supposed to be in it." And the town coordinator would be like, "No, you're not. You're not that guy. I'm sorry. Richie was mistaken."
<v Zac Pontier>这种事经常发生。每次镇协调员走到门廊上时，都会有更多的人在那里，就像：“你是镇协调员吗？我们在找镇协调员。里奇说我们在他的电影里有个大角色。在泳池边的那个场景，我就是那个应该出场的人。”而镇协调员会说：“不，你不是。你不是那个人。抱歉，里奇弄错了。”

00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:45.120
<v Zac Pontier>So they were all standing around like, "Oh, can I have your autograph then?" So I felt really bad for all these people that Richie just-- he didn't get it through his head that, for that time, from when they left where he was and drove to where the movie set, they thought he was the best guy in the world. He wasn't the person that said that they weren't going to be in the movie, he was the person that said they were. So it made him look like a really good guy, a really nice guy. I think that's why he did it. He liked people to think he was a really good guy.
<v Zac Pontier>所以他们都站在那儿说：“哦，那我可以要你的签名吗？”所以我为所有这些人感到很难过，里奇只是——他没意识到，在那段时间里，从他们离开他所在的地方开车到电影片场时，他们都以为自己是世界上最棒的人。并不是那个告诉他们无法出演的人让他看起来很糟糕，而是那个说他们能出演的人。所以这让他看起来像个非常好的人，一个非常棒的人。我想这就是他这么做的原因。他喜欢让别人觉得他是个很好的人。

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:53.100
<v Alex Blumberg>Richie had misled people about who he was. He'd made promises he couldn't keep. But when you talk to him, you get the sense that he'd hooked himself along with everyone else.
<v Alex Blumberg>里奇误导了人们关于他是谁的信息。他许下了无法兑现的承诺。但当你和他交谈时，你会觉得他不仅骗了别人，连他自己也被骗了进去。

00:14:53.100 --> 00:15:32.740
<v Richard Castellano>I'd become what I put in my head, and I'd become that person. Like, my wife was just talking. We were watching a movie about Vietnam. And we're sitting there, and I said to my wife, "Yeah, you see that guy, he's in the foxhole? When I was in Vietnam, that's where I used to hide. And when it used to rain, we used to go there." She says, "You were never in Vietnam." I said, "I know," I said, "but it sounds good, don't it?" I find it very easy to do, to become somebody else, because sometimes, if I'm not happy with who I am, I could step into that character.
<v Richard Castellano>我成了我脑子里塑造的那个人，我就成了那个人。比如，我妻子刚才在谈话。我们正在看一部关于越南的电影。我们坐在那儿，我对我妻子说：“是啊，看到那个家伙了吗，他在掩体里？我以前在越南时，那就是我躲藏的地方。每当下雨时，我们就去那儿。”她说：“你从没去过越南。”我说：“我知道，”我说，“但听起来不错，对吧？”我发现成为别人非常容易，因为有时候，如果我不喜欢现在的自己，我就能走进那个角色里。

00:15:32.740 --> 00:15:55.390
<v Alex Blumberg>During one of Richie's many appearances in court, the prosecuting attorney asked him if he'd ever told anyone he'd committed murder. He said yes, he had. The attorney then asked if this was true, and he said no. He explained, "I live the roles I play in my movies."
<v Alex Blumberg>在里奇多次出庭期间，检察官问他是否曾告诉任何人他犯过谋杀罪。他说是的，他确实说过。律师随后问这是否属实，他说没有。他解释说：“我活在我在电影中扮演的角色里。”

00:15:55.390 --> 00:16:24.260
<v Alex Blumberg>The final straw came in August of 2000. For months, advertisements had announced that Four Deadly Reasons would premiere during the Second Annual Narrowsburg Film Festival. Zac Pontier rented a suit especially for the occasion, set up a video camera in the back of the theater to capture the whole event. Here's what he recorded. Joe Dinki, the screenwriter on the film, came out in front of the curtain, sat on the edge of the stage, dangled his legs over the side and, leaning back on one elbow, the microphone in his free hand, delivered this news.
<v Alex Blumberg>最后一根稻草出现在2000年8月。几个月来，广告一直宣称《四个致命理由》将在第二届纳罗斯堡电影节期间首映。扎克·庞蒂尔为了这个场合专门租了一套西装，还在剧院后排架设了一台摄像机记录整个过程。以下是他录下的内容。电影编剧乔·丁基（Joe Dinki）走到幕布前，坐在舞台边缘，双腿悬空，一只手撑着手肘，另一只手拿着麦克风，传达了这一消息。

00:16:24.260 --> 00:16:46.560
<v Joe Dinki>Everybody raise their hand, anyone remember those summers where everything just stinks? I wrote this screenplay for Four Deadly Reasons from an original story by Richard's son and another person. And we took it and we made it into a, I think, a very viable, very sellable, very fun, mob film. So let's all applaud Narrowsburg for their participation. Thank you.
<v Joe Dinki>大家都举起手来，有人记得那些一切都很糟糕的夏天吗？我根据理查德的儿子和另外一个人的原创故事，编写了《四个致命理由》的剧本。我们把它拿来，拍成了一部我认为非常可行、非常有卖点、非常有趣的黑帮片。所以让我们为纳罗斯堡的参与鼓掌。谢谢。

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:46.800
<v Joe Dinki>[APPLAUSE]
<v Joe Dinki>[掌声]

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:51.640
<v Audience>All right!
<v Audience>太好了！

00:16:51.640 --> 00:17:33.950
<v Joe Dinki>Now, the hard part. We don't have the whole film here today. And I really do apologize. But, what you will see is 15 minutes, sorry, of the film that shows exactly what we were doing, and how much fun it is, and how great your area looks, and all the people that were in this film. So, I don't want to belabor the point and keep apologizing because you guys are great, and I think it's a fun film. And I know it's going to go places. And I'm having one of those really bad summers.
<v Joe Dinki>现在，困难的部分来了。我们今天没能带来整部电影。我真的非常抱歉。但是，你们将看到15分钟——抱歉——这部电影的片段，它展示了我们正在做的东西，它有多有趣，你们的地区看起来有多棒，以及所有参与这部电影的人。所以，我不想纠结于这个问题，也不想一直道歉，因为你们都很棒，我认为这是一部有趣的电影。我知道它会取得成功的。而且我正经历着一个糟糕透顶的夏天。

00:17:33.950 --> 00:17:56.830
<v Alex Blumberg>What came next was a hastily produced, 15-minute montage of scenes from the movie. It was like a porn film without the sex scenes-- awkward dialogue, noodly soundtrack, gratuitous use of bikinis and double entendres, and, most offensive, no scenes with people from Narrowsburg. Laurie Stuart, who works at the local paper, said that when it ended, the audience was stunned.
<v Alex Blumberg>接下来是一个仓促制作的15分钟电影片段剪辑。就像一部没有性爱场景的色情片——尴尬的对话、无聊的配乐、无缘无故出现的比基尼和双关语，最令人反感的是，里面没有任何纳罗斯堡当地人的镜头。在当地报纸工作的劳里·斯图尔特（Laurie Stuart）说，当片段结束时，观众们惊呆了。

00:17:56.830 --> 00:18:29.930
<v Laurie Stuart>You just couldn't believe it. Nobody could believe it. And then, 175 people just were excused, I mean, literally, excused. The guy stood in front of the audience and said, "OK, that's it. You're excused." And 175 people, in disbelief, filed out of that theater. I mean, at that point, any good will that folks had was really just frittered away.
<v Laurie Stuart>你简直不敢相信。没人敢相信。然后，175个人就这样被“请”出去了，我是说，真的就是被请出去了。那个家伙站在观众面前说：“好的，就这样。请离开。”175个处于难以置信中的人，鱼贯走出了那家剧院。我的意思是，那一刻，大家之前的所有好感真的都被挥霍殆尽了。

00:18:29.930 --> 00:18:55.470
<v Alex Blumberg>After the premiere, people in town stopped treating Richie as a lovably outsized character and set their sights on revenge. Suddenly, everyone was suing him-- a rental car company, 14 people from his film crew, a half dozen of his acting students. Part of the reason they were upset has to do with the real reasons most of them got involved with Richie in the first place. And to understand those reasons, you have to visit someone like John Borg, the biggest investor in Castellano's film.
<v Alex Blumberg>首映后，镇上的人不再把里奇当作一个可爱但夸张的角色，而是把目光投向了报复。突然间，每个人都在起诉他——一家租车公司，14名剧组成员，半打表演班的学生。他们感到愤怒的部分原因，与他们当初参与里奇电影的真正原因有关。要理解这些原因，你必须去拜访像约翰·博格（John Borg）这样的人，他是卡斯特拉诺电影最大的投资人。

00:18:55.470 --> 00:19:11.540
<v John Borg>There is all my proceeds from my check, which I give him-- checks, $3,000 here, on 8/10/99, $5,000, 7/9/99.
<v John Borg>这是我给他的支票的所有记录，我给他——支票，这里是3000美元，99年8月10日，5000美元，99年7月9日。

00:19:11.540 --> 00:19:57.130
<v Alex Blumberg>John Borg lives in a double-wide trailer on a small farm off a gravel road in the woods. On top of his dresser, there's a handgun laying next to an English pronunciation dictionary. Chickens run free in his yard, and he supplements his social security income by selling their eggs. There's some dispute about just how much money Richard Castellano owes John Borg. Castellano admits to borrowing around $60,000. Borg says it's $154,000. Borg says that, in decades of marriage, he'd never gone out to eat once. He didn't invest in the stock market and, up until he met Castellano, he had his entire life savings in the bank. When I ask him why he loaned so much of his money-- by his account, his entire life savings-- to a man he barely knew, he goes to his living room, picks up a video off the top of the TV, and shows it to me.
<v Alex Blumberg>约翰·博格住在一辆双宽拖车里，位于树林里的一条碎石路旁的小农场上。他的梳妆台上放着一把手枪，旁边是一本英语发音词典。鸡在院子里自由地跑来跑去，他靠卖鸡蛋来补贴社会保障金。关于理查德·卡斯特拉诺欠约翰·博格多少钱，存在一些争议。卡斯特拉诺承认借了大约6万美元。博格说金额是15.4万美元。博格说，在几十年的婚姻生活中，他从没出去吃过一次饭。他不投资股市，在遇见卡斯特拉诺之前，他把一生的积蓄都存在银行里。当我问他为什么把这么多钱——据他说，是他毕生的积蓄——借给一个他几乎不认识的人时，他走到客厅，从电视机上方拿出一盘录像带给我看。

00:19:57.130 --> 00:20:20.980
<v John Borg>He said, "I made movie with Robert De Niro and Billy Crystal." I went to buy the movie. I bought it in Kmart in [? Honesdale. ?] I've got the movie right here, Analyze That .
<v John Borg>他说：“我和罗伯特·德尼罗和比利·克里斯托拍过电影。”我去买了那部电影。我在霍恩斯代尔（Honesdale）的凯马特（Kmart）买了它。电影就在这儿，《老大靠边闪》。

00:20:20.980 --> 00:20:24.440
<v Alex Blumberg>Did it sound suspicious to you at all, or not really?
<v Alex Blumberg>你有没有觉得有什么可疑的地方，还是说根本没有？

00:20:24.440 --> 00:20:40.820
<v John Borg>In the beginning, no, because he's a very great and a very sweet talker. And I believed the guy because of that stupid movie right there.
<v John Borg>起初没有，因为他是一个非常棒、非常甜言蜜语的家伙。因为那部该死的电影就在那儿，所以我相信了他。

00:20:40.820 --> 00:21:03.510
<v Alex Blumberg>In the end, one of the most surprising things about this story is just how little stardust Castellano was offering people, a bit role in a so-so Hollywood movie, and how far it took him. John Borg is like most people in Narrowsburg. He says he loaned Richie the money because he thought it would help bring people and business to the area, but you get the sense there's something more. I ask, did Richie ever offer to put him in the movie?
<v Alex Blumberg>归根结底，这个故事最令人惊讶的事情之一是卡斯特拉诺提供给人们的星光是多么微不足道——一部平庸好莱坞电影里的一个小角色，却让他走得这么远。约翰·博格就像纳罗斯堡的大多数人一样。他说他借钱给里奇是因为他认为这能带动当地的生意和人气，但你会有种感觉，还有别的原因。我问他，里奇有没有提出让他演电影？

00:21:03.510 --> 00:21:11.110
<v John Borg>"Well," he said, "we're going to see. Maybe going to get you some part." I told him I'm not interested.
<v John Borg>“嗯，”他说，“我们到时候看吧。也许能给你安排个角色。”我告诉他我没兴趣。

00:21:11.110 --> 00:21:13.060
<v Alex Blumberg>But part of you was a little interested?
<v Alex Blumberg>但你内心其实有一点感兴趣，对吧？

00:21:13.060 --> 00:21:19.260
<v John Borg>Well, if I would be interested, I would have play Marshal Dillon, which I got a picture right there.
<v John Borg>嗯，如果我有兴趣的话，我就演马歇尔·迪伦（Marshal Dillon），那张照片就在那儿。

00:21:19.260 --> 00:21:21.180
<v Alex Blumberg>Marshal Dillon, what do you mean?
<v Alex Blumberg>马歇尔·迪伦，你是什么意思？

00:21:21.180 --> 00:21:28.930
<v Ira Glass>At this point, John Borg goes and picks up a framed photograph, resting on a doily on top of his VCR, of a figure with a cowboy hat and a handgun.
<v Ira Glass>此时，约翰·博格走过去，拿起一张放在录像机上花边垫子里的装裱照片，照片上是一个戴着牛仔帽、拿着手枪的人物。

00:21:28.930 --> 00:21:29.200
<v Alex Blumberg>So, this is--
<v Alex Blumberg>所以，这是——

00:21:29.200 --> 00:21:30.370
<v John Borg>Yeah, that's me. Yes.
<v John Borg>是啊，那是我。是的。

00:21:30.370 --> 00:21:31.340
<v Alex Blumberg>That's you?
<v Alex Blumberg>那是你？

00:21:31.340 --> 00:21:33.400
<v John Borg>Yeah. And that's my gun too.
<v John Borg>是的。那也是我的枪。

00:21:33.400 --> 00:21:35.640
<v Alex Blumberg>Uh-huh, and you've got a cowboy hat. Is that at your house?
<v Alex Blumberg>嗯哼，你还有顶牛仔帽。这是在你家吗？

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:37.080
<v John Borg>I got my hat here in the closet.
<v John Borg>我的帽子在壁橱里。

00:21:37.080 --> 00:21:41.570
<v Alex Blumberg>Yeah. This is the part you wanted to play? Wait, wait.
<v Alex Blumberg>是啊。这是你想扮演的角色吗？等一下，等一下。

00:21:41.570 --> 00:21:57.270
<v John Borg>Not much, you know, because we didn't talk too much about it. But he told me that he going to give me a guy going to be like a law enforcement officer. So it was Marshal Dillon. Well, everything [? ran  up for the end, ?] so what can I do?
<v John Borg>没多少，你知道，因为我们没聊太多。但他告诉我，他要给我一个角色，就像执法官那样。所以就是马歇尔·迪伦。嗯，一切都（？到最后），那我能做什么呢？

00:21:57.270 --> 00:22:18.880
<v Alex Blumberg>The more time I spend in Borg's house, the more evidence I discover of his obsession with the movies. He owns dozens of videos in big shopping bags, and on shelves, and stacked on tables. Later in the conversation, fairly abruptly in the middle of a story, he gets up, grabs his cowboy hat, turns the brim up like Soupy Sales and announces that he can act, if that's what you call this.
<v Alex Blumberg>我在博格家里待的时间越长，我就越发现他痴迷电影的证据。他有几十盘录像带装在大购物袋里、架子上，堆在桌子上。在谈话的后期，他在讲故事讲到一半时，突然起身，抓起牛仔帽，把帽檐翻起来，学着苏皮·塞尔斯（Soupy Sales）的样子宣布，如果这就是所谓表演的话，那他也能演。

