00:26:08.620 --> 00:27:20.330
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, David Sedaris swears he took a bath, really, despite what the tourists say. That's in a minute from Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来的节目，大卫·塞达里斯发誓他洗过澡了，真的，尽管那些游客们不是这么说的。稍后我们将从国际公共广播电台继续我们的节目。

00:27:20.330 --> 00:27:37.400
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose some theme, bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Our show today, The Three Kinds of Deception. Act One, if you'll recall, was about self-deception. We've arrived at Act Two, Deceiving Others.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，当然，我们都会选择一个主题，为您带来各种不同类型的相关故事。我们今天的节目主题是“三种欺骗”。如果你们还记得的话，第一幕是关于“自欺欺人”。我们现在来到了第二幕，“欺骗他人”。

00:27:37.400 --> 00:28:03.580
<v Ira Glass>When he was 30 years old, a Harvard Law School graduate and a practicing attorney making over $100,000 a year, Lawrence Otis Graham decided to take a job as a busboy at an all-white country club. To be clear, he didn't decide to do this because of his own experience at these kinds of clubs, though he'd had some. Growing up on the border of Scarsdale, New York, there were white families all around that belonged to country clubs, though his family, a well-to-do black family from Memphis, did not.
<v Ira Glass>劳伦斯·奥蒂斯·格雷厄姆（Lawrence Otis Graham）毕业于哈佛大学法学院，是一名年薪超过10万美元的执业律师。30岁时，他决定去一家全白人乡村俱乐部当勤杂工。需要说明的是，他做这个决定并不是因为他自己在这种俱乐部里的经历，尽管他确实有过一些。他在纽约州斯卡斯代尔（Scarsdale）郊区边缘长大，周围有许多白人家庭都属于不同的乡村俱乐部，而他的家庭——一个来自孟菲斯的富裕黑人家庭——却没有。

00:28:03.580 --> 00:28:24.100
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>My very first club experience was not a very positive one. It was when I was around seven years old. My parents objected to my brother and I going to a country club with our best friends who lived across the street. And they belonged to a club that my parents said did not permit blacks or Jews, so they brought us to the club because we insisted we wanted to go. I was seven. My brother was nine.
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>我第一次去俱乐部的经历并不是很愉快。那是大约我7岁的时候。我的父母反对我和哥哥去街对面的好朋友参加的乡村俱乐部。他们参加的俱乐部，我父母说是不允许黑人或犹太人进入的，但我们坚持要去，所以他们带我们去了。我7岁，我哥哥9岁。

00:28:24.100 --> 00:28:47.810
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>But we got into the children's swimming pool. They had a kiddie pool and then an adult pool. And, within less than two or three minutes, all of the kids got out of the pool. And my brother, Richard, and I ran out of the pool too because we thought there was something in the water that was going to hurt us. And then we realized this was a club that just did not want us there.
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>但我们进了儿童游泳池。他们有一个浅水池，然后是一个成人泳池。不到两三分钟，所有的孩子都从池子里出来了。我和哥哥理查德也跑出了池子，因为我们以为水里有什么东西会伤害我们。然后我们意识到，这是一个根本不想让我们待在那儿的俱乐部。

00:28:47.810 --> 00:29:08.670
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>My parents had warned me that something might happen, but my brother and I were growing up in what we imagined to be liberal and informed New York suburban communities where we thought that the stories that my parents told us about Memphis and the other parts of the South from the '40s and '50s didn't exist anymore.
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>父母曾警告过我可能会发生这种事，但我哥哥和我是在我们自以为开明和知情的纽约郊区社区长大的，我们以为父母告诉我们的那些关于40年代和50年代孟菲斯以及南方其他地区的故事已经不复存在了。

00:29:08.670 --> 00:29:17.420
<v Ira Glass>I just think about your parents in that situation, of how protective and sad they must have felt for you. But also, at the same time, they must have felt like, well, it's good that you saw it.
<v Ira Glass>我只是在想你的父母在那种情况下，该有多么保护你，又该有多么难过。但与此同时，他们一定觉得，好吧，你能看到这些也是件好事。

00:29:17.420 --> 00:29:32.300
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>Right. They felt it was good that we saw it. They understood the environment. They knew that nothing, physically, would happen to us. But it was an important lesson for me to learn at that age.
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>对。他们认为让我们看到是件好事。他们了解环境。他们知道我们不会受到身体伤害。但对我那个年龄段的孩子来说，这是一个重要的教训。

00:29:32.300 --> 00:29:40.900
<v Ira Glass>Many years later, as an adult working at a fancy Manhattan law firm, Lawrence Otis Graham and his friends all noticed something.
<v Ira Glass>许多年后，作为曼哈顿一家高级律师事务所的成年员工，劳伦斯·奥蒂斯·格雷厄姆和他的朋友们都注意到了某件事。

00:29:40.900 --> 00:30:13.840
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>Many friends who were working in New York, who were black, or Latino, or Jewish, or female, kept sharing stories with me that, in their firms, they were noticing that the people that were moving up had-- in their businesses-- were ones who were brought to country clubs over the weekend. And I started to notice that there was a pattern, that none of us-- none of the blacks, none of the Jews, none of the women, none of the Latinos or Asians-- were being brought.
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>许多在纽约工作的黑人、拉丁裔、犹太人或女性朋友经常和我分享故事，说在他们的事务所里，他们注意到那些获得晋升的人——在他们的业务中——都是周末被带到乡村俱乐部的人。我开始注意到有一个模式，即我们之中没有一个人——无论是黑人、犹太人、女性、拉丁裔还是亚裔——被邀请过去。

00:30:13.840 --> 00:30:28.390
<v Ira Glass>Again, to be clear about his motivations, he wanted to be part of that network. He wanted access to those connections. And he was curious about what, exactly, went on at these exclusive clubs. And that's why he decided to get in the only way that he could, as a busboy.
<v Ira Glass>再次说明，他动机很明确，他想成为那个网络的一部分。他想要获得那些人脉资源。而且他很好奇，这些排外的俱乐部里到底在进行什么活动。这就是他决定通过他唯一能进去的方式——作为一名勤杂工——进去的原因。

00:30:28.390 --> 00:30:49.040
<v Ira Glass>For two weeks, he left his high-paid job at the law firm and went undercover, essentially, a black man impersonating a black man. He chose the oldest, most prestigious club he could get to, the 100-year-old Greenwich Country Club, in patrician, white Greenwich, Connecticut. It turned out to be hard work, physically demanding work. And he wrote up this account of his experience.
<v Ira Glass>两周时间里，他离开了律师事务所的高薪工作，实际上是进行了卧底，一个黑人伪装成另一个黑人。他选择了对他来说能进入的最古老、最负盛名的俱乐部，即在贵族气息浓厚的康涅狄格州格林威治，拥有100年历史的格林威治乡村俱乐部。这证明是一项辛苦的工作，对体力要求很高。他写下了这段经历。

00:30:49.040 --> 00:31:12.980
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>March 28 to April 7, 1992. I invented a completely new resume for myself. I erased Harvard, Princeton, and my upper-middle-class suburban childhood from my life. I pieced together a wardrobe with a polyester blazer, ironed blue slacks, black loafers, and a horrendous pink, black, and silver tie, and I set up interviews at clubs.
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>1992年3月28日至4月7日。我为自己编造了一份全新的简历。我从生活中删除了哈佛、普林斯顿以及我的中上阶层郊区童年。我拼凑了一套行头：一件涤纶西装、烫过的蓝色长裤、黑色平底鞋以及一条恐怖的粉色、黑色加银色领带，然后去俱乐部参加面试。

00:31:12.980 --> 00:31:34.010
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>Over the telephone, five of the eight said that I sounded as if I would make a great waiter. But when they saw that I was black, the club managers told me I would quote, "probably make a much better busboy." "Busboy? Over the phone you said you needed a waiter," I argued.
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>通过电话，八个面试单位中有五个说我听起来是个理想的服务员人选。但当他们看到我是黑人时，俱乐部经理告诉我，我“可能更适合当一名勤杂工”。“勤杂工？电话里你不是说需要服务员吗？”我争辩道。

00:31:34.010 --> 00:32:00.720
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>Sunday, April 12. Today was my first day at work. Looking in my wallet, I removed my American Express Gold Card, my Harvard Club membership ID, and all of my business cards. When I arrived at the club, my new boss waved me in. "Good morning, Larry," he said with a sufficiently warm smile. "Hi, Mr. Ryan. How's it going?" Glancing at his watch to check my punctuality, he shook my hand and handed me some papers.
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>4月12日，星期日。今天是我第一天上班。我翻了翻钱包，拿走了我的美国运通金卡、哈佛俱乐部会员证和所有的名片。我到达俱乐部时，我的新老板向我招手。“早上好，拉里（Larry），”他带着一种足够温暖的微笑说道。“嘿，赖安（Ryan）先生。最近怎么样？”他看了一眼手表查看我是否准时，和我握了握手，递给我一些表格。

00:32:00.720 --> 00:32:49.410
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>"Oh, and by the way, where'd you park?" "In front, near the tennis courts." Already shaking his head, he tossed his pencil onto the desk. "That's off-limits to you. You should always park in the back, enter in the back, and leave from the back. No exceptions." "I'll do the forms right now," I said. "And then, I'll be an official busboy." Mr. Ryan threw me an ominous nod. "And Larry, let me stop you now. We don't like that term busboy. We find it demeaning. We prefer to call you busmen." Leading me down the center stairwell to the basement, he added, "And in the future, you will always use the back stairway by the back entrance."
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>“哦，顺便问一下，你把车停哪儿了？”“在前面，靠近网球场的地方。”他摇了摇头，把铅笔扔到桌子上。“那是禁区。你总是应该停在后面，从后面进入，从后面离开。没有例外。”“我马上填完表格，”我说。“然后，我就正式成为一名勤杂工了。”赖安先生给了我一个不祥的点头。“还有拉里，我得纠正你一下。我们不喜欢‘勤杂工’这个词。我们觉得这有贬低的意思。我们更喜欢叫你们‘巴士男’（Busmen）。”他带我走下中央楼梯来到地下室，补充道：“以后，你要永远使用后门附近的后楼梯。”

00:32:49.410 --> 00:33:36.030
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>He showed me the Mixed Grill, a well-lit pastel room with glass French doors and white wood trim. "Guys, say hello to Larry. He's a new busman at the club." I waved. "And this is Rick, Stephen, Drew, Buddy, and Lee." Five white waiters dressed in white polo shirts with blue 1892 club insignia nodded while busily slicing lemons. "And this is Hector and Carlos, the other busmen." Hector, Carlos, and I were the only non-whites on the serving staff. They greeted me in a mix of English and Spanish. "Nice to meet all of you," I responded. "Thank God," one of the taller waiters cried out. "Finally, somebody who can speak English."
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>他向我展示了“混合烧烤厅”，一个光线充足、带有法式玻璃门和白色木饰的粉彩房间。“伙计们，跟拉里打个招呼。他是俱乐部新的巴士男。”我挥了挥手。“这是里克（Rick）、斯蒂芬（Stephen）、德鲁（Drew）、巴迪（Buddy）和李（Lee）。”五名穿着白色Polo衫、带有蓝色1892年俱乐部徽章的白人服务员一边忙着切柠檬，一边点头。“这是赫克托（Hector）和卡洛斯（Carlos），另外两名巴士男。”赫克托、卡洛斯和我是在服务人员中仅有的非白人。他们用英语和西班牙语混合着向我打招呼。“很高兴见到你们，”我回应道。“谢天谢地，”其中一名较高的服务员大喊一声。“终于有个会说英语的了。”

00:33:36.030 --> 00:34:04.160
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>Standing outside the ice room, Carlos and I talked about our pasts. He was 25, originally from Colombia, and hadn't finished school. "You going to live in the monkey house?" Carlos asked. "What's that?" We climb the stairs to take our 10-minute lunch break before work begin. "Monkey house is where workers live here," Carlos said.
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>站在冰库外面，卡洛斯和我谈论了我们的过去。他25岁，来自哥伦比亚，没念完书。“你要住进猴子屋吗？”卡洛斯问。“那是什么？”我们爬上楼梯，在工作开始前进行10分钟的午休。“猴子屋就是这里员工住的地方，”卡洛斯说。

00:34:04.160 --> 00:34:28.310
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>I was soon back downstairs working in the grill. At my first few tables, I tried to avoid making eye contact with members as I removed dirty plates and wiped down tables and chairs. I was sure I'd be recognized. At around 1:15, four men who looked to be in their mid to late 50s sat down at a six-chair table while pulling off their cotton windbreakers and golf sweaters.
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>我很快又回到楼下在烧烤厅工作。在最初的几桌，我尽量避免与会员进行眼神接触，忙着撤下脏盘子，擦拭桌椅。我肯定会被认出来的。大约1点15分，四个看起来50多岁的人坐在一张有六把椅子的桌旁，脱下了他们的棉质风衣和高尔夫毛衣。

00:34:28.310 --> 00:34:50.150
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>"It's these damned news people that cause all these problems," said golfer number one, shoving his hand deep into a popcorn bowl. "These Negroes wouldn't even be thinking about golf. They can't afford to join a club anyway." Golfer number two squirmed out of his navy blue sweater and nodded in agreement. "My big problem with this Clinton fellow is that he apologized."
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>“就是这些该死的新闻媒体造成了所有这些问题，”一号高尔夫球手说，把手伸进爆米花碗里。“这些黑鬼（Negroes）甚至不会想到打高尔夫球。反正他们也负担不起加入俱乐部的费用。”二号高尔夫球手挣脱了他的海军蓝毛衣，点头表示同意。“我和这个克林顿家伙的问题是，他道歉了。”

00:34:50.150 --> 00:35:21.670
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>As I stood watching from the corner of the bar, I realized the men were talking about Governor Bill Clinton's recent apology for playing at an all-white golf club in Little Rock, Arkansas. "Hope, I couldn't agree with you more," added golfer number three, a hefty man who was biting off the end of a cigar. "You got any iced tea?" golfer number one asked as I put the silverware and menus around the table. Popcorn flew out of his mouth as he attempted to speak and chew at the same time. "Yes, we certainly do."
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>当我站在酒吧角落观察时，我意识到这些人在谈论比尔·克林顿州长最近因在阿肯色州小石城一家全白人高尔夫俱乐部打球而作出的道歉。“霍普，我完全同意，”三号高尔夫球手附和道，他是个大块头，正咬着雪茄的末端。“你有冰茶吗？”当我把餐具和菜单摆在桌边时，一号高尔夫球手问道。他边说话边嚼东西，爆米花从嘴里喷了出来。“是的，我们当然有。”

00:35:21.670 --> 00:35:54.320
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>Golfer number three removed a beat-up Rolex from his wrist. "It just sets a bad precedent. Instead of apologizing, he should try to discredit them, undercut them somehow. What's to apologize for?" I cleared my throat and backed away from the table. Suddenly, golfer number one waved me back to his side. "Should we get four iced teas, or just a pitcher and four glasses?" "I'd be happy to bring whatever you'd like, sir."
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>三号高尔夫球手从手腕上摘下一块破旧的劳力士表。“这开了一个坏先例。他本应该尝试诋毁他们，设法从内部瓦解他们，而不是道歉。有什么可道歉的？”我清了清嗓子，退出了桌边。突然，一号高尔夫球手挥手让我回到他身边。“我们是要四杯冰茶，还是直接来一扎和四个杯子？”“我很乐意为您带来任何您想要的，先生。”

00:35:54.320 --> 00:36:32.970
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>"Oh, busboy," a voice called out as I made the rounds with two pots of coffee. "Here, busboy. Here, busboy," the woman called out. "Busboy, my coffee is cold. Give me a refill." "Certainly. I would be happy to." I reached over for her cup. The 50-ish woman pushed her hand through her straw blond hair and turned to look at me in the face. "Decaf, thank you." "You are quite welcome." Before I turned toward the kitchen, the woman leaned over to her companion. "My goodness, did you hear that? That busboy has diction like an educated white person."
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>“哦，勤杂工，”当我带着两壶咖啡巡视时，一个声音喊道。“这儿，勤杂工。这儿，勤杂工，”那个女人喊道。“勤杂工，我的咖啡凉了。给我续杯。”“当然，我很高兴为您续杯。”我伸手去拿她的杯子。那个50岁左右的女人撩了一下她那草莓金色的头发，转头看着我的脸。“无咖啡因，谢谢。”“不客气。”在我转向厨房之前，那个女人向她的同伴倾身。“我的天哪，你听到了吗？那个勤杂工说话的语气就像个受过教育的白人。”

00:36:32.970 --> 00:37:03.810
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>A curly-haired waiter walked up to me in the kitchen. "Larry, are you living in the monkey house?" "No, but why do they call it that?" "Well, no offense against you, but it got that name since it's the house where the workers have lived at the club and since the workers used to be Negroes or blacks, it was nicknamed the monkey house. And the name just stuck, even though Negroes have now been replaced by Hispanics."
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>一个卷发服务员在厨房里向我走来。“拉里，你住在猴子屋吗？”“没有，但他们为什么叫它那个名字？”“嗯，不是针对你，但它得名于那是俱乐部员工住的地方，而且因为过去员工往往是黑人（Negroes），所以被称为猴子屋。这个名字就这么留了下来，尽管黑人现在已经被西班牙裔取代了。”

00:37:03.810 --> 00:37:38.920
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>Saturday, April 18. Today, while I was wiping down a table, I heard a member snap his fingers in my direction. I turned to see a group of young men smoking cigars. They seemed to be my age or a couple of years younger. "Hey, do I know you?" the voice asked. As I turned slowly toward the voice, I could hear my own heartbeat. I was sure it was someone I knew. "No," I said, approaching the blond cigar-smoker. "I don't think so." "You must be new. What's your name?" "My name is Larry. I just started a few days ago."
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>4月18日，星期六。今天，当我擦桌子时，听到一个会员冲我打了个响指。我转过身，看到一群抽雪茄的年轻男子。他们看起来和我同龄或者比我小几岁。“嘿，我认识你吗？”那个声音问。当我慢慢转过身向那个声音时，我能感觉到自己的心跳。我确定是我认识的人。“没有，”我说，走近那个抽雪茄的金发男。“我不这么认为。”“你一定是新来的。你叫什么名字？”“我叫拉里。我几天前刚开始工作。”

00:37:38.920 --> 00:38:19.880
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>The cigar-smoking host grabbed me by the wrist while looking at his guests. "Well, Larry, welcome to the club. I'm Mr. Billings, and this is Mr. Dennis, a friend and new member." "Hello, Mr. Dennis," I heard myself saying to a freckle-faced young man who puffed uncomfortably on his fat roll of tobacco. The first cigar-smoker gestured for me to bend over, as if he were about to share some important confidence. "Now, Larry, here's what I want you to do. Go get us some of those peanuts, and then give my guests and me a fresh ashtray. Can you manage that?"
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>那个抽雪茄的东道主抓住了我的手腕，同时看着他的客人们。“嗯，拉里，欢迎来到俱乐部。我是比林斯（Billings）先生，这是丹尼斯（Dennis）先生，一位朋友和新会员。”“你好，丹尼斯先生，”我听到自己对一个满脸雀斑的年轻人说，他正不舒服地吸着那根粗大的烟卷。第一个抽雪茄的人示意我弯下腰，好像要分享什么重要的秘密。“好了，拉里，这就是我要你做的。去给我们拿些花生，然后给我的客人们和我换个干净的烟灰缸。你能办到吗？”

00:38:19.880 --> 00:38:52.740
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>Tuesday, April 21. Just as we were all leaving for the day, Mr. Ryan came down to hand out the new policies for those who were going to be living in the monkey house. Since it had recently been renovated, the club was requiring all new residents to sign the form. The policy included a rule that forbade employees to have overnight guests. Rule 14 stated that the club management had the right to enter an employee's locked bedroom at any time, without permission, and without giving notice.
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>4月21日，星期二。就在我们所有人要下班离开时，赖安先生下来分发给那些要住进猴子屋的人的新政策。由于刚刚装修过，俱乐部要求所有新住户签署表格。政策包括一条禁止员工有留宿客人的规定。第14条规定，俱乐部管理层有权在未经许可、无需通知的情况下，随时进入员工上锁的卧室。

00:38:52.740 --> 00:39:47.110
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>As I was making rounds with my coffee pots, I overheard a raspy-voiced woman talking to a mother and daughter who were thumbing through a catalog of infants' clothing. "The problem with au pairs is that they're usually only in the country for a year." The mother and daughter nodded in agreement. "But getting one that's a citizen has its own problems. For example, if you ever have to choose between a Negro and one of these Spanish people, always go for the Negro." One of the women frowned, confused. "Really?" "Yes," the raspy-voiced woman responded with cold logic. "Even though you can't trust either one, at least Negroes speak English and can follow your directions." Before I could refill the final cup, the raspy-voiced woman looked up at me and smiled. "Oh, thanks for the refill, Larry."
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>当我带着咖啡壶巡视时，我无意中听到一个声音沙哑的女人正在和一个正在翻看婴儿服装目录的母女谈话。“互惠生的难题在于她们通常只在这个国家待一年。”母女俩点头表示同意。“但找个公民互惠生也有自己的麻烦。例如，如果你必须在黑人和这些西班牙人之间做选择，一定要选黑人。”其中一个女人困惑地皱起眉头。“真的吗？”“是的，”那个声音沙哑的女人以一种冰冷的逻辑回应。“虽然两者都不可信，但至少黑人会说英语，而且能听从你的指令。”在我还没来得及给最后一个杯子续完咖啡之前，那个声音沙哑的女人抬头看了看我，微笑着说：“哦，谢谢你的续杯，拉里。”

00:39:47.110 --> 00:40:04.680
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>Wednesday, April 22. Today, the TV was turned to testimony and closing arguments from the Rodney King police beating trial in California. "I am so sick of seeing that awful videotape," one woman said to friends at her table. "It shouldn't be on TV."
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>4月22日，星期三。今天，电视机正放着加利福尼亚罗德尼·金（Rodney King）警察殴打案的证词和结案陈词。“我真看够了那段可怕的录像带，”一个女人对桌边的朋友说。“那根本就不该在电视上播放。”

00:40:04.680 --> 00:40:46.860
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>At around 2:00, Lois, the club's official secretary, asked me to help her send out a mailing to 600 members after my shift. She took me up to her office on the main floor and introduced me to the two women who sat with her. "Larry, this is Marge, whom you'll talk with in three months because she's in charge of employee benefits." I smiled at the brunette. "And, Larry, this is Sandy, whom you'll talk with after you become a member at the club because she's in charge of members' accounts." Both Sandy and I looked up at Lois with shocked expressions. Lois winked and, at the same moment, the three jovial women burst out laughing.
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>大约2点左右，俱乐部的官方秘书洛伊斯（Lois）让我下班后帮她发送一份给600名会员的邮件。她把我带到她主楼的办公室，向我介绍了和她坐在一起的两个女人。“拉里，这是玛吉（Marge），你三个月后会和她谈，因为她负责员工福利。”我对着那个黑发女子笑了笑。“还有，拉里，这是桑迪（Sandy），你成为俱乐部会员后会和她谈，因为她负责会员账户。”桑迪和我看着洛伊斯，脸上露出了震惊的表情。洛伊斯眨了眨眼，与此同时，这三个开朗的女人爆发出大笑。

00:40:46.860 --> 00:41:18.030
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>Thursday, April 22. At the end of the day, Mr. Ryan handed me my first paycheck. When I opened the envelope and saw what I'd earned, $174.04 for five days, I laughed out loud. Back in the security of a bathroom stall, where I had periodically been taking notes since my arrival, I studied the check and thought about how many hours and how hard I'd worked for so little money. It was less than 1/10 of what I'd make in the same time in my law firm.
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>4月22日，星期四。下班时，赖安先生给了我第一份工资支票。当我打开信封看到我的收入——五天174.04美元——时，我大笑出声。回到卫生间的隔间里，自从我到达这里后，我就一直定期在那里记笔记，我仔细端详了这张支票，想过我为了这么点钱投入了多少时间和多么辛苦的工作。这不到我在律师事务所同样时间内赚取收入的十分之一。

00:41:18.030 --> 00:42:01.800
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>I went upstairs and asked Mr. Ryan about my paycheck. "Well, we decided to give you $7 an hour," he said. I'd never actually been told my hourly rate. "But if the check looks especially big, Larry, that's because you got some extra pay in there for all of your terrific work on Good Friday. And by the way, Larry, don't tell any of the others what you're getting because we're giving you a special deal, and it's really nobody else's business." I nodded and thanked him for his largess. I stuffed some more envelopes, emptied out my locker, and left.
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>我上楼去询问赖安先生关于我的工资支票的事。“嗯，我们决定给你每小时7美元，”他说。我从没被告知过我的时薪。“但如果支票看起来特别大，拉里，那是因为你在耶稣受难日（Good Friday）的出色工作得到了一些额外报酬。顺便说一句，拉里，不要告诉其他人你拿多少，因为我们给了你一个特殊待遇，而且这真的不关别人的事。”我点了点头，感谢他的慷慨。我装好更多的信封，清空了储物柜，然后离开了。

00:42:01.800 --> 00:42:23.420
<v Ira Glass>Lawrence Otis Graham, reading from one of the essays in his book, Member of the Club . He says he wasn't surprised at what he called the passive bigotry of the club members, who, he said, did not think of themselves as racist. Interestingly, the more pointed language came from club staff who routinely used the word nigger.
<v Ira Glass>劳伦斯·奥蒂斯·格雷厄姆，摘自他书中的一篇散文《俱乐部成员》。他说，他对俱乐部成员所谓的被动偏见并不感到惊讶，这些人说，他们并不认为自己是种族主义者。有趣的是，更尖锐的语言来自俱乐部员工，他们经常使用“黑鬼”（nigger）这个词。

00:42:23.420 --> 00:42:48.310
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>It was not a reference to me, but it was in reference to them watching television. The television was constantly on, so it was, there those niggers are again kind of thing. But, two seconds later, they would turn to me and say, "Oh, Larry, can you come, do so-and-so?" I honestly don't even believe that they thought that people would have been offended by the use of the term.
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>那不是指我，而是指他们在看电视时说的。电视一直开着，所以会说，又有那些黑鬼了。但两秒钟后，他们会转向我说：“哦，拉里，你能过来做下那个谁吗？”我甚至不相信他们认为人们会因为使用这个词而感到冒犯。

00:42:48.310 --> 00:42:51.170
<v Ira Glass>When you heard this, were you surprised at all?
<v Ira Glass>当你听到这个时，你感到惊讶吗？

00:42:51.170 --> 00:42:52.530
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>No. No.
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>不，不。

00:42:52.530 --> 00:42:53.160
<v Ira Glass>Not at all?
<v Ira Glass>一点也不？

00:42:53.160 --> 00:43:05.990
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>I'll tell you why I wasn't surprised. I wasn't surprised because of what they had put me through in order to get the job. I had three call-back interviews for this job, three call-back interviews for a busboy job.
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>我告诉你我为什么不惊讶。我不惊讶是因为他们为了让我得到这份工作而让我经历的事情。我有三次回访面试，为了一个勤杂工的工作，有三次面试。

00:43:05.990 --> 00:43:07.180
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:43:07.180 --> 00:43:27.320
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>I mean, that is outrageous. If you look at what it would take to get a job at IBM, I don't think you'd have three call-back jobs. So it was very clear to me there was something about myself that made them feel uncomfortable.
<v Lawrence Otis Graham>我的意思是，这太离谱了。如果你看看在IBM找份工作需要什么，我觉得你都不需要三次面试。所以很清楚，我身上有什么东西让他们感到不舒服。

00:43:27.320 --> 00:43:34.740
<v Ira Glass>We called the Greenwich Country Club to find out if the club now admits blacks, or Jews, or any minority members at all. The club declined to respond.
<v Ira Glass>我们致电格林威治乡村俱乐部询问该俱乐部现在是否接纳黑人、犹太人或任何少数族裔成员。俱乐部拒绝回应。

00:43:34.740 --> 00:43:58.700
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "CHEAP LABOR" BY NEO BOYS]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - NEO BOYS的《廉价劳动力》]

00:43:58.700 --> 00:44:10.340
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, Accidental Deception. OK, to review, thus far on our program we have witnessed self-deception, the willful deception of others, and how one of those can lead to the other.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，意外的欺骗。好的，总结一下，到目前为止，我们的节目已经见证了自欺欺人、对他人蓄意的欺骗，以及这两者是如何互相转化的。

00:44:10.340 --> 00:44:21.660
<v Ira Glass>David Sedaris is a writer living, these days, in Paris, France, with his boyfriend, Hugh. He now has this story of accidental deception and how it can send somebody down the slippery slope to both of the other kinds.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·塞达里斯是一位作家，目前与他的男朋友休（Hugh）住在法国巴黎。他现在有一个关于意外欺骗的故事，以及它是如何把一个人推向通往其他两种欺骗的滑坡的。

00:44:21.660 --> 00:44:43.880
<v David Sedaris>It was July, and Hugh and I were taking the Paris Metro from our neighborhood to a store where we hoped to buy a good deal of burlap. During the summer months, a great number of American vacationers can be found riding the Metro, and their voices tend to carry. It's something I hadn't noticed until leaving home, but we are a loud people.
<v David Sedaris>那是七月，休和我在巴黎坐地铁去一家我们希望买到大量粗麻布的商店。夏季月份里，许多美国度假者会乘坐地铁，他们的嗓门通常很大。这是我在离开家之前没注意到的，但我们确实是个吵闹的民族。

00:44:43.880 --> 00:45:11.470
<v David Sedaris>On the first of our two trains, I listened to a quartet of college-age Texans who sat beneath a sign instructing passengers to surrender their seats and stand, should the foyer of the train become too crowded. The foyer of the train quickly became too crowded. And, while the others stood to make more room, the young Texans remained seated and raised their voices in order to continue their debate, the topic being which is the better city, Houston or Paris?
<v David Sedaris>在我们搭乘的两趟列车中的第一趟上，我听着四个德克萨斯大学的学生在一块标识下聊天，那个标识指示乘客在列车门厅过于拥挤时让出座位并站立。列车门厅很快变得过于拥挤。而在其他人站起来腾出空间时，那几个年轻的德州学生仍然坐着，提高嗓门继续他们的辩论，主题是休斯顿还是巴黎哪座城市更好？

00:45:11.470 --> 00:45:17.080
<v David Sedaris>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Sedaris>[笑声]

00:45:17.080 --> 00:45:42.510
<v David Sedaris>It was a hot afternoon, and the subject of air conditioning came into play. Houston had it, Paris did not. Houston also had ice cubes, tacos, plenty of free parking, and something called a Sonic burger. Things were not looking good for Paris, which lost valuable points every time the train stopped to accept more passengers.
<v David Sedaris>那是一个炎热的下午，空调的话题随之而来。休斯顿有空调，巴黎没有。休斯顿还有冰块、塔可、充足的免费停车位，以及一种叫“索尼克”（Sonic）的汉堡。巴黎的情况看起来很不妙，列车每停靠一次接受新乘客，巴黎就会扣掉宝贵的分数。

00:45:42.510 --> 00:46:03.220
<v David Sedaris>The crowds packed in, surrounding the seated Texans and reducing them to four disembodied voices. From the far corner of the car, one of them shouted that they were tired and dirty and ready to catch the next plane home. The voice was weary and hopeless, and I identified completely. It was the same way I'd felt on my last visit to Houston.
<v David Sedaris>拥挤的人群包围了坐着的德州人，把他们缩减成四个没身体的声音。在车厢的最角落，其中一个人喊道，他们很累，很脏，准备搭下一班飞机回家了。声音充满了疲惫和绝望，我完全感同身受。和我上次去休斯顿时的感觉一模一样。

00:46:03.220 --> 00:46:05.570
<v David Sedaris>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Sedaris>[笑声]

00:46:05.570 --> 00:46:38.200
<v David Sedaris>Hugh and I disembarked to the strains of "Texas, Our Texas," and boarded our second train, where an American couple in their late 40s stood hugging the floor-to-ceiling support pole. There's no sign saying so, but such poles are not considered private. They're put there for everyone's use. You don't treat it like a fireman's pole. Rather, you grasp it with one hand and stand back at a respectable distance. It's not all that difficult to figure out, even if you come from a town without any public transportation.
<v David Sedaris>休和我伴随着《德克萨斯，我们的德克萨斯》的歌声下车，登上了第二趟列车，一对40岁出头的美国夫妇正紧抱着那根从地板到天花板的支撑杆。没有标识说，但这种杆子不被认为是私人的。它们是供所有人使用的。你不要把它当成消防员的杆子。相反，你应该一只手握住它，站在保持礼貌距离的地方。这并不难弄明白，即使你来自一个没有任何公共交通的城镇。

00:46:38.200 --> 00:46:52.980
<v David Sedaris>The train left the station and, needing something to hold onto, I wedged my hand between the American couple and grabbed the pole at waist level. The man then turned to the women saying, "PU, can you smell that? That is pure French, baby."
<v David Sedaris>列车离开车站，因为需要抓住什么东西保持平衡，我把手插在那对美国夫妇中间，抓住了腰部的杆子。那个男人转过身对女人说：“PU（臭死了），你闻到了吗？那可是纯粹的法国味儿，宝贝。”

00:46:52.980 --> 00:46:58.150
<v David Sedaris>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Sedaris>[笑声]

00:46:58.150 --> 00:47:40.710
<v David Sedaris>He removed one of his hands from the pole and waved it back and forth in front of his face. "Yes, indeed," he said, "this little froggie is ripe." It took a moment to realize he was talking about me. The woman wrinkled her nose. "Golly, Pete," she said, "do they all smell this bad?" "It's pretty typical," the man said. "I'm willing to bet that our little friend here hasn't had a bath in a good two weeks. I mean, Jesus Christ, someone should hang a deodorizer around this guy's neck." The woman laughed, saying, "You crack me up, Martin. I swear, you do."
<v David Sedaris>他把一只手从杆子上移开，在脸前扇了扇。“没错，”他说，“这只小青蛙（little froggie）真够臭的。”我花了一会儿才意识到他是在说我。那个女人皱了皱鼻子。“天哪，皮特，”她说，“他们都这么臭吗？”“很正常，”男人说。“我敢打赌我们这个小哥儿们两周没洗过澡了。我的意思是，天哪，真应该在他脖子上挂个除臭剂。”那个女人笑着说：“你真逗，马丁。我发誓，你真幽默。”

00:47:40.710 --> 00:47:45.630
<v David Sedaris>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Sedaris>[笑声]

00:47:45.630 --> 00:48:23.970
<v David Sedaris>It's a common mistake for vacationing Americans to assume that everyone around them is French and, therefore, speaks no English whatsoever. An experienced traveler could've told by looking at my shoes that I wasn't French. And, even if I were French, it's not as if English is some mysterious tribal dialect spoken only by anthropologists and a small population of cannibals. Because they had used the tiresome word froggie and complained about my odor, I was now licensed to hate this couple as much as I wanted. This made me happy, as I'd wanted to hate them from the moment I'd entered the subway car.
<v David Sedaris>度假的美国人通常会有一个共同的错误假设，即周围的每个人都是法国人，因此根本不会说英语。一个有经验的旅行者看一眼我的鞋子就能知道我不是法国人。而且，即使我是法国人，英语也不是什么只有人类学家和少数食人族才会说的神秘部落方言。因为他们使用了“青蛙”这个讨厌的词，还抱怨我的气味，所以我现在有理由尽情讨厌这对夫妇了。这让我很开心，因为从我进入车厢的那一刻起，我就想讨厌他们。

00:48:23.970 --> 00:48:24.950
<v David Sedaris>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Sedaris>[笑声]

00:48:24.950 --> 00:48:27.870
<v David Sedaris>[APPLAUSE]
<v David Sedaris>[掌声]

00:48:27.870 --> 00:48:48.990
<v David Sedaris>Unleashed by their insults, I was now free to criticize Martin's clothing-- the pleated denim shorts, the baseball cap, the t-shirt advertising a San Diego pizza restaurant. Glasses hung from his neck on a fluorescent cable, and the couple's bright, new, his-and-hers sneakers suggested that they might be headed somewhere dressy for dinner.
<v David Sedaris>在他们侮辱的刺激下，我现在可以自由地批评马丁的穿着了——褶皱牛仔短裤、棒球帽、印着圣地亚哥披萨店广告的T恤。眼镜挂在他脖子上的一根荧光绳上，那对夫妇崭新的、情侣款的运动鞋暗示着他们可能正打算去什么正式的地方吃晚餐。

00:48:48.990 --> 00:48:51.700
<v David Sedaris>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Sedaris>[笑声]

00:48:51.700 --> 00:48:57.800
<v David Sedaris>Comfort has its place, but it seems rude to visit another country dressed as if you've come to mow its lawns.
<v David Sedaris>舒适感固然重要，但打扮得像去割草一样去另一个国家旅行似乎不太礼貌。

00:48:57.800 --> 00:49:03.950
<v David Sedaris>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Sedaris>[笑声]

00:49:03.950 --> 00:49:25.690
<v David Sedaris>People are often frightened of Parisians, but an American in Paris will find no harsher critic than another American. France isn't even my country, but there I was, deciding that these people needed to be sent back home, preferably in chains. In disliking them, I was forced to recognize my own pretension, and that made me hate them even more.
<v David Sedaris>人们通常害怕巴黎人，但美国人在巴黎找不到比另一个美国人更严厉的批评者了。法国甚至不是我的国家，但我却在那里决定这些人需要被送回家，最好是带着镣铐。在讨厌他们的过程中，我被迫认清了自己的自命不凡，这让我更加讨厌他们了。

00:49:25.690 --> 00:49:28.990
<v David Sedaris>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Sedaris>[笑声]

00:49:28.990 --> 00:49:54.060
<v David Sedaris>The train took a curve and, when I moved my hand further up the pole, the man turned to the woman, saying, "Carol. Hey, Carol, watch out. That guy's going after your wallet." "What?" "Your wallet," Martin said, "that joker's trying to steal your wallet. Move your pocketbook to the front where he can't get at it." She froze. And he repeated himself, barking, "The front! Move your pocketbook around to the front! Do it now! The guy's a pickpocket!"
<v David Sedaris>列车转弯时，当我的手向上移到杆子上时，男人转向女人说：“卡罗尔（Carol）。嘿，卡罗尔，小心。那家伙要偷你的钱包。”“什么？”“你的钱包，”马丁说，“那个小丑正试图偷你的钱包。把你的皮包移到前面去，他够不到的地方。”她僵住了。他重复了一遍，咆哮道：“前面！把你的包移到前面来！现在就做！那家伙是个扒手！”

00:49:54.060 --> 00:49:57.570
<v David Sedaris>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Sedaris>[笑声]

00:49:57.570 --> 00:50:31.440
<v David Sedaris>The woman named Carol grabbed for the strap on her shoulder and moved her pocketbook so that it now rested upon her stomach. "Wow," she said. "I sure didn't see that coming." "Well, you've never been to Paris before, but let that be a lesson to you." Martin glared at me. His eyes narrowed to slits. "This city is full of stinkpots like our little friend here. Let your guard down and they'll take you for everything you've got." Now I was a stinkpot and a thief.
<v David Sedaris>那个名叫卡罗尔的女人抓住肩带，把皮包移到肚子前。“哇，”她说。“我确实没料到会有这出。”“好吧，你以前没来过巴黎，但让这成为给你的一个教训。”马丁盯着我，眼睛眯成一条缝。“这个城市充满了像我们这个小哥儿们这样的臭东西。一旦放下戒备，他们就会骗走你所有的东西。”现在我成了“臭东西”和“小偷”。

00:50:31.440 --> 00:51:02.030
<v David Sedaris>It occurred to me to say something, but I thought it might be better to wait and see what he came up with next. Besides, if I said something at this point, he probably would have apologized and I wasn't interested in that. His embarrassment would have pleased me, but once he recovered, there would be that awkward period that sometimes culminates in a handshake. I didn't want to touch these people's hands or see things from their point of view. I just wanted to continue hating them, and so I kept my mouth shut and stared off into space.
<v David Sedaris>我本想说点什么，但我觉得最好等等看他接下来会出什么招。再说，如果我现在开口，他可能会道歉，而我对此不感兴趣。他的尴尬会让我感到愉悦，但一旦他恢复过来，就会出现那种尴尬的停顿，有时甚至以握手告终。我不想触碰这些人的手，也不想从他们的角度看问题。我只想继续讨厌他们，所以我闭上嘴，两眼空洞地望着虚空。

00:51:02.030 --> 00:51:36.660
<v David Sedaris>The train stopped at the next station. Passengers got off. And Carol and Martin moved to occupy two folding seats located beside the door. I thought they might ease onto another topic, but Martin was on a roll now, and there was no stopping him. "It was some [BLEEP] head like him that stole my wallet on my last trip to Paris," he said, nodding his head in my direction. "He got me on the subway, came up from behind, and I never felt a thing. Cash, credit cards, driver's license, poof, all of it gone, just like that." I pictured a scoreboard reading Marty, zero; stinkpots, one.
<v David Sedaris>列车在下一站停下。乘客下了车。卡罗尔和马丁移到门边的两个折叠椅上坐下。我原以为他们会换个话题，但马丁现在兴致正高，根本停不下来。“上次去巴黎时，就是某个像他一样的[消音]头偷了我的钱包，”他点头示意我这边。“他在地铁上抓住了我，从背后接近，我一点感觉都没有。现金、信用卡、驾照，砰，全没了，就像那样。”我脑海里出现了一个比分牌：马蒂，零；臭东西，一。

00:51:36.660 --> 00:51:39.150
<v David Sedaris>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Sedaris>[笑声]

00:51:39.150 --> 00:52:35.540
<v David Sedaris>"What you've got to understand is that these creeps are practiced professionals," he said. "I mean, they've really got it down to an art, if you can call that an art form." "I wouldn't call it an art form," Carol said. "Art is beautiful, but taking people's wallets, that stinks, in my opinion." "You've got that right," Martin said. "The thing is that these jokers usually work in pairs." He squinted toward the opposite end of the car. "Odds are that he's probably got a partner somewhere on this subway car." "You think so?" "I know so," he said. "They usually time it so that one of them clips your wallet just as the train pulls into the station. The other guy's job is to run interference and trip you up once you catch wind of what's going on. Then the train stops, the doors open, and they disappear into the crowd. If stinky there had gotten his way, he'd probably be halfway to Timbuktu by now. I mean, make no mistake, these guys are fast."
<v David Sedaris>“你必须明白的是，这些混蛋是经验丰富的职业选手，”他说。“我是说，他们真的把这变成了一门艺术，如果那也能叫艺术的话。”“我不称之为艺术，”卡罗尔说。“艺术是美的，但偷别人的钱包，在我看来简直臭不可闻。”“你说得对，”马丁说。“关键是，这些小丑通常是搭档行事的。”他斜着眼向车厢的另一端看去。“可能性很大，他在这辆地铁上还有个同伙。”“你这么认为？”“我敢肯定，”他说。“他们通常算好时间，在列车进站的一瞬间下手。另一个人的工作是负责阻挠和绊住你，一旦你意识到发生了什么事。然后列车停下，门打开，他们就消失在人群中。如果‘臭东西’得逞的话，他现在可能已经在去廷巴克图（Timbuktu）的路上了。我的意思是，别搞错了，这些家伙动作很快。”

00:52:35.540 --> 00:53:20.350
<v David Sedaris>I'm not the sort of person normally mistaken for being fast and well-coordinated, and, because of this, I found Martin's assumption to be oddly flattering. "It just gets my goat," he said. "I mean, where's the polizioni when you need one?" Polizioni? Where did he think he was? I tried to imagine Martin's conversation with a French policeman, and pictured him waving his arms shouting, "That man tried to pick-a my friend's pocketoni!" I wanted very much to hear such a conversation, and so I decided I would take the wallet from Hugh's back pocket as we left the train.
<v David Sedaris>我通常不是那种会被误认为动作迅速、协调性好的人，正因为如此，我发现马丁的臆测竟然有点让人受宠若惊。“这简直让我火大，”他说。“我的意思是，需要警察的时候他们在哪里？”警察（Polizioni）？他以为他在哪儿？我试图想象马丁与法国警察的对话，幻想着他挥舞着手臂喊道：“那个人试图偷我朋友的口袋（pocketoni）！”我非常想听到这样的对话，所以我决定在离开列车时从休的后口袋里拿走钱包。

00:53:20.350 --> 00:53:22.540
<v David Sedaris>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Sedaris>[笑声]

00:53:22.540 --> 00:53:26.380
<v David Sedaris>[APPLAUSE]
<v David Sedaris>[掌声]

00:53:26.380 --> 00:53:55.990
<v David Sedaris>Martin would watch me steal from a supposed stranger and would most likely intercede. He'd put me in a headlock or yell for help. And, when a crowd gathered, I'd say, "What's the problem? Is it against the law to borrow money from my own boyfriend?" If the police came, he would explain the situation in his perfect French while I'd toss in a few of my most polished phrases. "That guy's crazy," I'd say, pointing at Martin. "I think he's drunk. Look at how his face is swollen."
<v David Sedaris>马丁会看着我偷一个所谓的陌生人，很可能会干预。他会给我来个锁喉或者大声求救。然后，当人群聚集时，我会说：“有什么问题吗？向我自己的男朋友借钱也犯法吗？”如果警察来了，他会用他那完美的法语解释情况，而我会插进几句我最精炼的词组。“那家伙疯了，”我会说，指向马丁。“我觉得他喝醉了。看他脸肿的。”

00:53:55.990 --> 00:54:22.410
<v David Sedaris>I was practicing these lines to myself when Hugh came up from behind and tapped me on the shoulder, signaling that the next stop was ours. "There you go," Martin said. "That's him. That's the partner. Didn't I tell you he was around here somewhere? They always work in pairs. It's the oldest trick in the book." Hugh had been reading the paper and had no idea what had been going on. It was too late now to pretend to pick his pocket, and I was stuck without a decent backup plan.
<v David Sedaris>我正对着自己练习这些台词时，休从后面走过来拍了拍我的肩膀，示意下一站就是我们要下的。 “看吧，”马丁说。“那就是他。那是同伙。我不是告诉过你他就在附近吗？他们总是搭档行事。这是这行里最古老的伎俩了。”休一直在看报纸，根本不知道发生了什么。现在再假装偷他的钱包已经太晚了，我被困住了，没有了像样的后备计划。

00:54:22.410 --> 00:54:40.130
<v David Sedaris>As we pulled into the station, I recalled an afternoon 10 years earlier. I'd been riding the Chicago El with my sister, Amy, who was getting off two or three stops ahead of me. The doors opened. And, as she stepped out of the crowded car, she turned around to yell, "So long, David. Good luck beating that rape charge."
<v David Sedaris>列车进站时，我想起了十年前的一个下午。我和妹妹艾米（Amy）坐着芝加哥的轨道列车，她比我早下两三站。门开了。当她走出拥挤的车厢时，她转过身大喊：“再见，大卫。祝你打赢那场强奸官司好运。”

00:54:40.130 --> 00:54:53.000
<v David Sedaris>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Sedaris>[笑声]

00:54:53.000 --> 00:55:08.530
<v David Sedaris>Everyone on board had turned to stare at me. Some seemed curious, some seemed frightened, but the overwhelming majority appeared to hate me with a ferocity I had never before encountered. "That's my sister," I said.
<v David Sedaris>车上所有人都转过头盯着我。有些人显得好奇，有些人显得惊恐，但绝大多数人似乎带着一种我从未遇到过的凶狠态度憎恨我。“那是我妹妹，”我说。

00:55:08.530 --> 00:55:13.330
<v David Sedaris>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Sedaris>[笑声]

00:55:13.330 --> 00:55:30.630
<v David Sedaris>"She likes to joke around." I laughed and smiled, but it did no good. Every gesture made me appear more guilty. And I wound up getting off at the next stop rather than continue riding alongside people who thought of me as a rapist.
<v David Sedaris>“她喜欢开玩笑。”我笑了笑，但这没用。每一个动作都让我看起来更加罪有应得。最后，我不得不在下一站下车，而不是继续与那些认为我是强奸犯的人坐在一起。

00:55:30.630 --> 00:56:11.370
<v David Sedaris>I wanted to say something that good to Martin, but I can't think as fast as Amy. In the end, this man would go home warning his friends to watch out for pickpockets in Paris. He'd be the same old Martin, but, at least for the next few seconds, I still had the opportunity to be somebody different, someone quick and dangerous. The new and dangerous me noticed how Martin tightened his fists when the train pulled to a stop. Carol held her pocketbook close against her chest and sucked in her breath as Hugh and I stepped out of the car, no longer finicky little boyfriends on their overseas experiment, but rogues, accomplices, halfway to Timbuktu.
<v David Sedaris>我想对马丁说一些同样精彩的话，但我没法像艾米那样思维敏捷。最终，这个男人会回到家警告他的朋友们在巴黎要当心扒手。他还是那个老马丁，但至少在接下来的几秒钟里，我还有机会成为一个不同的人，一个机敏而危险的人。那个崭新的、危险的我注意到列车进站时马丁是如何握紧拳头的。卡罗尔紧紧抱着皮包压在胸前，屏住呼吸，休和我走出了车厢，不再是海外实验中挑剔的小男友，而是流氓，同伙，已经走在去往廷巴克图的一半路上了。

00:56:11.370 --> 00:56:12.360
<v David Sedaris>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Sedaris>[笑声]

00:56:12.360 --> 00:56:16.320
<v David Sedaris>[APPLAUSE]
<v David Sedaris>[掌声]

00:56:16.320 --> 00:56:46.520
<v Ira Glass>David Sedaris, reading an excerpt from his book Me Talk Pretty One Day .
<v Ira Glass>大卫·塞达里斯，摘自他书中的片段《讲得漂亮的那一天》（Me Talk Pretty One Day）。

00:56:46.520 --> 00:57:01.260
